<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Hamburg (1701)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1701</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> (1701) settled the succession dispute that followed the extinction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustrow">Güstrow</a> line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-mecklenburg">House of Mecklenburg</a> in 1695. Signed on 8 March 1701 under imperial mediation, it divided Mecklenburg between Duke Frederick William, who received Mecklenburg-Schwerin, and Adolphus Frederick II, who received the smaller Mecklenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strelitz">Strelitz</a>. The two duchies kept a joint diet and remained separate states until 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-hamburg-1701.jpg" alt="Treaty of Hamburg (1701)" width="320" height="478" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: die Vertragsparteien · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hamburger_Vergleich_1701.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
